  "post": {
    "title": "How long to Proxima Centauri? It depends on your clock 🚀",
    "description": "Proxima Centauri is 4.24 light-years away. Voyager 1, moving at about 17 km/s, would need about 75,000 years. Breakthrough Starshot's probes are designed for about 15% of light speed: about 28 years. For the crew, the clock on board runs slower: at nearly the speed of light (a Lorentz factor of about 223,000), a 4.24 light-year trip takes about 10 minutes of ship time.\n\nSources:\nNASA Science, Sun facts: https://science.nasa.gov/sun/facts/\nNASA Science, Voyager 1: https://science.nasa.gov/mission/voyager/voyager-1/\nBreakthrough Initiatives, Starshot: https://breakthroughinitiatives.org/initiative/3\nLorentz factor (time dilation): https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lorentz_factor\n\nNarration uses a synthetic voice.",
